    {
      "page_title": "Graceful Pod Shutdown",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q8. How does a Pod shut down gracefully (SIGTERM, terminationGracePeriodSeconds, preStop)?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "The kubelet runs ==preStop==, sends **SIGTERM**, waits out the grace period, then SIGKILLs.",
            "Default `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`: 30s",
            "App must catch SIGTERM, finish in-flight work",
            "Pod leaves Service endpoints at shutdown start"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "lifecycle:",
            "  preStop:",
            "    exec: {command: [sleep, '5']}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "Delete Pod",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              {
                "text": "preStop",
                "ink": "purple"
              },
              {
                "text": "SIGTERM",
                "ink": "orange"
              },
              {
                "text": "SIGKILL",
                "ink": "red"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "Key takeaways",
          "text": "Pod = scheduling unit; init once, sidecars stay; phase = Pod, state = container; catch SIGTERM before SIGKILL."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "kubernetes",
      "subtitle": "The smallest unit"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Deployments and ReplicaSets",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q9. What is the difference between a Pod, a ReplicaSet and a Deployment?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "A ==Pod== runs containers, a ReplicaSet keeps N copies, a Deployment manages ReplicaSets.",
            "Bare Pod: not recreated if deleted",
            "ReplicaSet: self-heals to desired count",
            "Deployment: adds rolling updates, rollback"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "kubectl get deploy,rs,pods",
            "# deploy/web",
            "# rs/web-7d4b9c",
            "# pod/web-7d4b9c-x2k8p"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q10. How does a rolling update work and what do maxSurge and maxUnavailable control?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "A ==new ReplicaSet== scales up while the old one scales down.",
            "**maxSurge**: extra Pods above desired",
            "**maxUnavailable**: Pods below desired",
            "Both default 25%; numbers or percentages"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "strategy:",
            "  rollingUpdate:",
            "    maxSurge: 1",
            "    maxUnavailable: 0"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q11. How do you roll back a bad Deployment and how is revision history kept?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "`kubectl rollout undo` points back at the ==previous ReplicaSet==.",
            "Old ReplicaSets stay at 0 replicas",
            "`revisionHistoryLimit` caps them, default 10",
            "`--to-revision=N` picks a specific one"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "kubectl rollout history deploy/ui",
            "kubectl rollout undo deploy/ui",
            "  --to-revision=2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q12. What is the difference between a Deployment, a StatefulSet and a DaemonSet?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Each fits a workload: ==stateless==, ==stateful==, or ==one per node==.",
            "Deployment: interchangeable, scales freely",
            "StatefulSet: stable names, own volumes",
            "DaemonSet: follows nodes"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Kind",
            "Pods",
            "Use"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Deployment",
              "Random names",
              "Web, APIs"
            ],
            [
              "StatefulSet",
              "db-0, db-1",
              "Databases, Kafka"
            ],
            [
              "DaemonSet",
              "One per node",
              "Log agents, CNI"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "Key takeaways",
          "text": "Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods. Surge/unavailable set pace; undo reuses old ReplicaSet."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "kubernetes",
      "aside": "Deployment owns ReplicaSets",
      "subtitle": "Rollouts and controllers"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Services and Ingress",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q13. What is the difference between ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer and ExternalName Services?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Each type widens the Pods' reach, or aliases DNS.",
            "==ClusterIP== internal-only, the default",
            "**NodePort** opens one port per node",
            "==LoadBalancer== adds cloud LB; ExternalName: CNAME"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "kind: Service",
            "spec:",
            "  type: NodePort  # 30000-32767",
            "  ports: [{port: 80}]"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q14. How does a Service find its Pods and route traffic to them (selectors, Endpoints, kube-proxy)?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "The ==selector== matches Pod labels; ready Pod IPs become Endpoints.",
            "EndpointSlices list only **ready** Pods",
            "kube-proxy builds iptables or IPVS rules",
            "ClusterIP traffic is DNAT-ed to one Pod"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "Client Pod",
                "sub": "my-svc:80",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              {
                "text": "ClusterIP",
                "sub": "virtual IP",
                "ink": "purple"
              },
              {
                "text": "kube-proxy",
                "sub": "iptables/IPVS",
                "ink": "orange"
              },
              {
                "text": "Pod IP",
                "sub": "Endpoints",
                "ink": "green"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q15. What is the difference between an Ingress and a LoadBalancer Service?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "A LoadBalancer Service gives one external IP per Service, layer 4.",
            "==Ingress== is layer 7: host/path routing",
            "Needs an **Ingress controller**, e.g. NGINX",
            "Also handles TLS termination"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "kind: Ingress",
            "spec:",
            "  rules:",
            "  - host: api.example.com"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q16. What is a headless Service and when would you use one?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "==clusterIP: None== makes DNS return Pod IPs directly.",
            "No virtual IP, no kube-proxy balancing",
            "Gives StatefulSet Pods stable DNS names",
            "Use when clients must pick a Pod"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "kind: Service",
            "spec:",
            "  clusterIP: None",
            "  selector: {app: db}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "Key takeaways",
          "text": "A Service is a stable name over changing Pods. Ingress shares one entry; headless skips the virtual IP."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "kubernetes",
      "aside": "stable IP, moving Pods",
      "subtitle": "Routing traffic in"
    },
