    {
      "page_title": "Volumes and Persistent Storage",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q21. What is the difference between emptyDir, hostPath and a PersistentVolume?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "An ==emptyDir== dies with its Pod, ==hostPath== mounts a node directory, and a ==PersistentVolume== is cluster storage that outlives Pods.",
            "emptyDir: scratch space, deleted when the Pod goes",
            "hostPath: pins data to one node, security risk",
            "PV: separate lifecycle, survives Pod and node loss"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "volumes:",
            "- name: cache",
            "  emptyDir: {}",
            "  # hostPath: {path: /data}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q22. How do PersistentVolume, PersistentVolumeClaim and StorageClass work together?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "A ==StorageClass== defines how storage is provisioned, a ==PVC== requests it, and a ==PV== is the real volume bound to that claim.",
            "PVC asks for size and access mode",
            "StorageClass provisions a matching PV dynamically",
            "Binding is **one-to-one**: one PVC, one PV"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "kind: PersistentVolumeClaim",
            "spec:",
            "  storageClassName: fast",
            "  accessModes: [ReadWriteOnce]"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q23. What are access modes and reclaim policies, and how do they affect your data?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "__Access modes__ say how many nodes can mount a volume, and the ==reclaim policy== decides what happens to the PV after its claim is deleted.",
            "RWO: one node; ROX: many read-only; RWX: many writers",
            "**Delete** wipes the backing disk; Retain keeps it",
            "Dynamic PVs default to Delete, so check first"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "# keep data after PVC delete",
            "spec:",
            "  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy:",
            "    Retain"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q24. How do StatefulSets give Pods stable identity and storage?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "A ==StatefulSet== gives each Pod a sticky ordinal name and its own PVC from `volumeClaimTemplates`, so identity and data survive rescheduling.",
            "Pods named db-0, db-1, db-2 start in order",
            "A headless Service gives each Pod stable DNS",
            "Each PVC stays when its Pod is rescheduled"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "serviceName: db",
            "volumeClaimTemplates:",
            "- metadata: {name: data}",
            "# creates PVC data-db-0"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "Key takeaways",
          "text": "Pods are disposable, claims are not: ask with a PVC, provision with a StorageClass, and check reclaim policy before deleting."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "database",
      "aside": "data outlives pods",
      "subtitle": "Q21 to Q24"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Scheduling",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q25. How does the scheduler decide which node a Pod runs on?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "==Filters== out unfit nodes, ==scores== survivors, binds the best.",
            "Filter: CPU/memory, taints, selectors, ports",
            "Score: spread, image locality, free resources",
            "Ties broken at random"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "Pending Pod",
                "ink": "orange"
              },
              {
                "text": "Filter",
                "sub": "drop unfit",
                "ink": "red"
              },
              {
                "text": "Score",
                "sub": "0-100",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              {
                "text": "Bind",
                "sub": "nodeName",
                "ink": "green"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q26. What is the difference between nodeSelector, node affinity and pod anti-affinity?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "`nodeSelector` is plain label matching; affinity adds operators, soft rules.",
            "Node affinity: In, NotIn, Exists; required/preferred",
            "Anti-affinity spreads replicas; needs topologyKey"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "nodeSelector:",
            "  disktype: ssd",
            "# affinity: In/NotIn/Exists",
            "# plus 'preferred' soft rules"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q27. What are taints and tolerations and how do they differ from affinity?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "A ==taint== repels Pods; only a matching ==toleration== lets them land.",
            "Taints push away; affinity pulls toward",
            "NoSchedule, PreferNoSchedule, NoExecute",
            "Toleration permits, never forces"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "tolerations:",
            "- key: gpu",
            "  operator: Exists",
            "  effect: NoSchedule"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q28. What is the difference between resource requests and limits, and what are QoS classes?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "**Requests** are scheduler-reserved; **limits** are the runtime ceiling.",
            "Scheduling uses requests, never actual usage",
            "Over CPU limit throttles; over memory OOM-kills"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "QoS class",
            "Rule",
            "Evicted"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Guaranteed",
              "request = limit",
              "last"
            ],
            [
              "Burstable",
              "some set",
              "middle"
            ],
            [
              "BestEffort",
              "nothing set",
              "first"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 0
        }
      ],
      "hero": "kubernetes",
      "aside": "filter, then score",
      "subtitle": "Placement and resources"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Probes and Autoscaling",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q29. What is the difference between liveness, readiness and startup probes?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Each asks the kubelet a different question: ==liveness== restart?, ==readiness== send traffic?, ==startup== booted?",
            "Liveness failure **restarts** container",
            "Readiness failure leaves Endpoints",
            "Startup holds other probes"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "startupProbe:",
            "  httpGet: {path: /healthz, port: 8080}",
            "  failureThreshold: 30"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Probe",
            "On failure",
            "Use for"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Liveness",
              "Restarted",
              "Deadlocks"
            ],
            [
              "Readiness",
              "Pulled from Service",
              "Warm-up"
            ],
            [
              "Startup",
              "Restarted at limit",
              "Slow boot"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q30. How does the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler calculate the desired replica count?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "HPA scales replicas by observed metric over target, rounded up.",
            "Metrics: metrics-server or adapters",
            "10% tolerance stops flapping",
            "Bounded by min/maxReplicas"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "desired = ceil(cur * val/target)",
            "4 pods, 90% vs 60%: 6 pods"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "metrics-server",
                "sub": "CPU 90%",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              {
                "text": "HPA",
                "sub": "90/60 = 1.5",
                "ink": "purple"
              },
              {
                "text": "Deployment",
                "sub": "4 → 6",
                "ink": "green"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q31. What is the difference between HPA, VPA and the Cluster Autoscaler?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "HPA adds Pods, VPA resizes Pods, Cluster Autoscaler adds nodes.",
            "HPA and VPA: not both on CPU",
            "Cluster Autoscaler reacts to **Pending** Pods",
            "VPA recreates Pods to resize"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Tool",
            "Scales",
            "Trigger"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "HPA",
              "Pod count",
              "Metric vs target"
            ],
            [
              "VPA",
              "Pod requests",
              "Usage"
            ],
            [
              "Cluster Autoscaler",
              "Nodes",
              "Unschedulable Pods"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 2
        }
      ],
      "hero": "kubernetes",
      "aside": "restart vs route!",
      "subtitle": "Health checks and scale"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Memory vs CPU Limits",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q32. What happens when a container exceeds its memory limit versus its CPU limit?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Memory is incompressible so it is killed; CPU is compressible so it is slowed.",
            "Memory over limit: **OOMKilled**, exit 137",
            "CPU over limit: throttled, never killed",
            "Throttling shows as latency"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Resource",
            "Over limit",
            "Symptom"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Memory",
              "OOM kill",
              "Restarts"
            ],
            [
              "CPU",
              "CFS throttling",
              "Slow responses"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "Key takeaways",
          "text": "Liveness restarts, readiness gates traffic. HPA: ceil(cur × val/target). Memory kills, CPU throttles."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "kubernetes",
      "subtitle": "Health checks and scale"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "RBAC and Networking",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q33. How does RBAC work: Role, ClusterRole, RoleBinding, ClusterRoleBinding?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "RBAC grants ==verbs on resources== through bindings.",
            "Roles hold rules",
            "Bindings grant them",
            "ClusterRoleBinding spans **all** namespaces"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Object",
            "Scope",
            "Does"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Role",
              "1 namespace",
              "Rules"
            ],
            [
              "ClusterRole",
              "Cluster",
              "Reusable rules"
            ],
            [
              "RoleBinding",
              "1 namespace",
              "Role to subject"
            ],
            [
              "ClusterRoleBinding",
              "All",
              "ClusterRole to subject"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q34. What is a ServiceAccount and how do Pods use it for the API server?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "A ==ServiceAccount== is a Pod identity whose token authenticates API calls.",
            "Default unless set",
            "Kubelet mounts rotated token",
            "Bind least-privilege Role"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "serviceAccountName: app-sa",
            "automountServiceAccountToken: true"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q35. How do NetworkPolicies work and what if none exist?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "A NetworkPolicy ==allow-lists== traffic for selected Pods; with none, all is **allowed**.",
            "Unlisted traffic denied",
            "Needs enforcing CNI (Calico)",
            "Empty `podSelector` selects all"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "kind: NetworkPolicy",
            "podSelector: {}",
            "policyTypes: [Ingress]"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q36. How does Pod-to-Pod networking work and what does a CNI do?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Each Pod has its own IP, reaching others ==without NAT== via CNI.",
            "Kubelet calls CNI: veth, IP, routes",
            "VXLAN or BGP crosses nodes"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "Pod A",
                "sub": "10.0.1.5",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              {
                "text": "veth+bridge",
                "ink": "purple"
              },
              {
                "text": "VXLAN/BGP",
                "ink": "orange"
              },
              {
                "text": "Pod B",
                "sub": "10.0.2.7",
                "ink": "green"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "hero": "shield-check",
      "aside": "least privilege!",
      "subtitle": "Q33 to Q36"
